      "side": 1,
      "message": "My main concern with this new logic is that it assumes obmc-chassis-poweron@0.target always finishes last. If someone ever had a config where the chassis 0 target finished before the leaf nodes, we would falsely say power is On when sysStateChangeJobRemoved() fires. Ideally we would be looking at everything in this 1 function (all leaf nodes and the state of obmc-chassis-poweron@0.target).\n\nThe assumption is ok because we have the wait-for-smp-poweron.service in the obmc-chassis-poweron@0.target but it feels like a bad assumption to code in here with the logic now being in 2 different functions.\n\nNeed to find a way to get it all in this 1 function. Maybe we just have this function add the state of obmc-chassis-poweron@0.target into it\u0027s logic. A separate class variable that only tracks the state of obmc-chassis-poweron@0.target that this function could look at? (set/clear the variable within sysStateChangeJobRemoved()) and have sysStateChangeJobRemoved() call this aggregatePowerState() whenever it changes?",
